                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_614">Cardiganshire. Llanbadarn
                        Fawr</orgName><p>This attribution was first added in the Medieval Libraries
                        of Great Britain Supplement (1987), along with the following footnote:
                           <q>see M. Lapidge, <title>Studia Celtica</title>, viii/ix (1973–74),
                           70–6.</q> According to Parkerweb's summary of this article: <q>The article
                           is on the Welsh-Latin Poetry linked with Sulien (1011-91), Bishop of St.
                           David's and Ieuan (d. 1137) the younger of Sulian's two sons. CCCC MS 199
                           was written at Llanbadarn Fawr and contains Ieuan's poem, <title>Carmen
                              de vita et familia Sulgeni</title> and two others by him. There is an
                           edition of these texts with English translation and extensive notes. The
                           other text in the ms, Augustine, <title>De Trinitate</title> is written
                           in Ieuan's own hand</q>.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_477">St David's,
                        Pembrokeshire, Cathedral church of St David</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was accepted (albeit with a query) in the second edition of
                        Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). However, the attribution was
                        later rejected in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval
                        Libraries of Great Britain index card records the following information,
                        which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution: Written by John, son of
                        Sulgen and brother of Ricemarch, bishop of St David's 1088-96. Said to have
                        contained at end two letters to Matthew Parker from Bishop Davies of St
                        David's.</p></provenance>
               </history>
